---
import Docs from "../layouts/Docs.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.ts";
---

<Docs
  title="Why it is safe"
  description="Peer-to-peer between browser tabs, encrypted with Noise, identities that are public keys, nothing kept on a server."
>
  <h1>Why it is safe</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Your services travel between two browser tabs, encrypted end to end, to people whose
    identity is a key only they hold. The relay in the middle can neither read nor change them,
    and no server keeps them.
  </p>

  <h2 id="tabs">1. Sharing happens between browser tabs</h2>
  <p>
    The hub is <strong>your own browser tab</strong>. Guests connect to it, and every request a
    guest makes is answered by your tab, from your computer. There is no PeerShare server that
    receives your pages, stores your files, or holds a copy of your guest list:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>your key, your services and your members live in your browser's storage;</li>
    <li>each guest's key lives in their browser;</li>
    <li>when your tab closes, nothing is shared anymore, by anyone.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The servers involved only carry or serve public things: the PeerShare page itself
    (<code>peershare.sandclaw.ai</code>), the empty shell of the isolated origins
    (<code>*.s.sandclaw.ai</code>), and the relay, which forwards encrypted bytes.
  </p>

  <h2 id="encryption">2. Connections are encrypted end to end (Noise)</h2>
  <p>
    Before any request is sent, the two browsers run a
    <a href="https://noiseprotocol.org">Noise</a> handshake, the libp2p secure channel.
    Each side proves it holds the private key behind its peer ID, and the two derive session keys
    that only they know. Everything after that is encrypted and authenticated with those keys.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Through the relay</strong>, the relay sees only ciphertext. It cannot read your
      pages, cannot change them without the change being detected, and cannot pose as either
      side.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Over a direct WebRTC connection</strong>, the data does not pass through the relay
      at all. WebRTC encrypts it (DTLS), and the two sides exchanged their key fingerprints over
      the Noise channel first, so the direct connection belongs to the same two peers.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    This holds on any network, public Wi-Fi included: someone on the same network sees encrypted
    traffic to the relay or between two browsers, not your services.
  </p>

  <h2 id="peer-ids">3. A peer ID is a public key</h2>
  <p>
    Every browser on the mesh has an Ed25519 key pair. Its <strong>peer ID</strong> is not a
    name someone assigns: it is derived from, and contains, the public key. Two consequences:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Nobody can claim to be you.</strong> Connecting as your peer ID means completing
      the Noise handshake with your private key, which never leaves your browser. A guest's
      browser dials the peer ID written in the invite, so it knows it reached your tab, and not
      someone who copied the link or controls the relay.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Your tab knows who is asking.</strong> The identity of every incoming request is
      taken from what the encrypted connection proved, never from anything the caller says. A
      header claiming to be another peer is removed and rewritten at your tab.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="membership">4. Membership is a signed token, checked by your tab</h2>
  <p>
    When a guest joins, your hub gives them a <a href="https://www.biscuitsec.org">Biscuit</a>
    token signed with your key. It names your mesh and is <strong>bound to the guest's peer
    ID</strong>.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Your tab verifies it on its own, with no server to ask: your peer ID is your public key,
      which is the token's root key.
    </li>
    <li>
      A copied token is useless to anyone else: it only works on a connection that proved the
      guest's key.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Revoke</strong> takes effect at once: your tab refuses that guest's next request
      and ends their open live connections, even if their tab is still open.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="invites">5. Invites are single-use, short-lived, and kept out of server logs</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>An invite works <strong>once</strong>; the second person to join with it is refused.</li>
    <li>It expires after <strong>24 hours</strong>.</li>
    <li>
      Everything in it sits in the link's fragment (after <code>#</code>), which browsers do not
      send in requests. It does not show up in server logs or in the <code>Referer</code> header.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    If someone else opens a link before your guest does, your guest is told it was already used:
    you notice, and revoke the stranger.
  </p>

  <h2 id="isolation">6. A shared service cannot reach the guest's PeerShare page</h2>
  <p>
    A service is a web app; its pages are code. A guest runs it in its <strong>own
    origin</strong>, a random name under <code>s.sandclaw.ai</code>, never in the PeerShare
    page. The browser's same-origin policy then keeps them apart:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>the service cannot read the guest's PeerShare key, token or storage;</li>
    <li>it cannot read the PeerShare page, or other services' pages;</li>
    <li>
      it never sees the guest's token: the PeerShare page adds it after the request leaves the
      service's origin.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    httpeers measured this for its session origins in Chromium and Firefox: two sessions, and the
    page that opened them, could not see each other's storage, cookies or IndexedDB, nor read the
    parent page.
  </p>

  <h2 id="proxy">7. The local proxy only works for you, and only nearby</h2>
  <p>The <a href={u("/local-proxy")}>local proxy</a> is the one piece on your computer:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>it listens on <code>127.0.0.1</code> only, never on your network;</li>
    <li>
      it answers only the PeerShare page and pages served from your own computer; any other
      website gets <code>403</code>, and with <code>--key</code> a wrong key gets
      <code>401</code>;
    </li>
    <li>
      it reaches only this computer and your local network: <code>localhost</code>, private
      addresses, Tailscale addresses (<code>100.x</code>), <code>.local</code> names;
    </li>
    <li>a public address is refused, so it is never a gateway to the internet for your guests;</li>
    <li>your browser itself asks you before the page may reach it (local network access).</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="framing">8. Services that forbid embedding stay that way, unless you allow it</h2>
  <p>
    Many web apps tell browsers never to show them inside another page, with an
    <code>X-Frame-Options</code> header or a <code>frame-ancestors</code> rule in their
    Content-Security-Policy. It protects against <strong>clickjacking</strong>: a hostile site
    loads the app invisibly in a frame, lines it up under its own buttons, and tricks someone who
    is signed in to the app into clicking <em>Delete</em>, <em>Approve</em> or <em>Transfer</em>
    in it without knowing.
  </p>
  <p>
    A guest always sees your service inside a frame of the PeerShare page, so such a service
    shows the guest an error instead ("will not allow Firefox to display the page if another site
    has embedded it"). PeerShare respects that by default: the service card warns you that the
    service refuses to be embedded, and nothing else changes.
  </p>

  <h3>What "Show it even if it forbids embedding" does</h3>
  <p>The switch is in each service's settings, off until you turn it on. When it is on:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      your tab removes <code>X-Frame-Options</code> and the <code>frame-ancestors</code> rule
      from that service's answers <strong>on their way to your guests</strong>, and nothing
      else: every other rule of the app's Content-Security-Policy (where scripts may come from,
      where the page may connect) stays in force;
    </li>
    <li>
      the app itself is unchanged: at its own address (<code>http://localhost:…</code>), for you
      and for anyone who reaches it directly, it keeps its protection;
    </li>
    <li>
      in place of the app's rule, the guest's copy gets PeerShare's own: it runs at a random
      origin under <code>s.sandclaw.ai</code> that only pages on <code>sandclaw.ai</code> may
      frame (<code>frame-ancestors 'self' https://sandclaw.ai https://*.sandclaw.ai</code>), and
      that origin's service worker refuses page loads that come from anywhere else.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>What it gives up</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Who could clickjack a guest.</strong> With the app's rule, nobody can frame it.
      Without it, pages on <code>sandclaw.ai</code> can: the PeerShare page, and any other site
      under that domain. A website anywhere else still cannot, and cannot even learn the random
      origin a guest's copy runs at. So the risk is a page on <code>sandclaw.ai</code> that is
      compromised or turns hostile: it could frame a guest's copy of your service and trick
      <em>that guest</em> into clicking in it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Whose powers are at stake.</strong> A clickjacked click acts as the guest, with
      what the guest can do in your app: their own sign-in (the local proxy keeps each guest's
      cookies apart), not yours. A service that guests use without signing in, or as a
      read-only user, has little to lose; one where guests act with real privileges has more.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Not a new view into your app.</strong> The PeerShare page already carries every
      request and answer between the guest and your service; framing gives it, or anyone, no
      way to read the app's pages, which stay in their own origin (see
      <a href="#isolation">6</a>).
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Some apps also check by script that they are the top page, and may still refuse or behave
    oddly inside the frame. That is no risk, but the switch cannot fix it.
  </p>

  <h3>When to turn it on</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Fine:</strong> things you share to be looked at or worked on together, such as a dev
      server, a dashboard, documentation, a notebook, where a guest's tricked click could do
      little harm.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Think twice:</strong> admin panels, anything with one-click destructive or
      money-moving actions, and apps where your guests are signed in with real privileges. Give
      guests a restricted account there, or change the app to allow
      <code>frame-ancestors https://*.s.sandclaw.ai</code> itself, which keeps everyone else out.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="limits">What PeerShare does not protect against</h2>
  <div class="callout warn">
    <p>
      <strong>Your guests see what you share.</strong> Encryption protects the way there, not what
      a guest does with a page once they have it. Share only what you would show them.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>An invite in the wrong hands</strong> admits whoever opens it first. Send it to one
      person, and revoke anyone you don't recognise.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>The PeerShare page and the session shell are trusted code.</strong> They are served
      from <code>peershare.sandclaw.ai</code> and <code>*.s.sandclaw.ai</code>; the page runs
      with your key, and both handle your services unencrypted inside the browser. Use the
      published addresses, or host them yourself.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>A remote https service you share directly learns each guest's peer ID</strong>:
      PeerShare tells it which guest is asking (that is how a service on your computer gets a
      cookie jar per guest). It never sees their token.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>The relay can see that two peer IDs talk</strong>, when and how much, though not
      what. It can also refuse to carry traffic, which stops sharing but reveals nothing.
    </p>
  </div>
</Docs>
